### What does yield() actually do?

yield() is a static method on the Thread class. It tells the OS scheduler that the current thread is willing to give up its remaining CPU time slice so that other threads of equal or higher priority can run. The scheduler is free to ignore this hint entirely.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class YieldDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread.yield();
        // The OS scheduler MIGHT let another thread run.
        // Or it might continue with this thread immediately.
        // No guarantee either way. Behavior is platform-dependent.
    }
}
```

### Why is yield() unreliable?

Modern operating systems use preemptive scheduling. The OS decides when to switch threads based on priorities, time slices, and system load, regardless of yield() hints. On Linux with the CFS (Completely Fair Scheduler), yield() may effectively do nothing because the scheduler already ensures fairness. On Windows, yield() behavior differs between versions.

### What state does the thread enter after yield()?

The thread stays in RUNNABLE state. It never enters WAITING, TIMED_WAITING, or BLOCKED. This means it continues to consume a slot in the scheduler's run queue and may be immediately rescheduled.

### What are the better alternatives?

Every use case where yield() might be considered has a better alternative.

| Instead of | Use | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `while(!done) yield()` | `CountDownLatch.await()` | Parks thread at 0% CPU |
| `yield()` in spin loop | `Thread.onSpinWait()` (Java 9) | CPU PAUSE instruction, saves power |
| `yield()` for timing | `Thread.sleep(1)` or `LockSupport.parkNanos(1)` | Guaranteed minimum delay |
| `yield()` for condition | `BlockingQueue.take()` or `Condition.await()` | Proper condition signaling |
| `yield()` for fairness | `ReentrantLock(true)` | Guaranteed FIFO ordering |

### What about Thread.onSpinWait() (Java 9)?

onSpinWait() is the modern replacement for yield() in spin-wait loops. It emits a PAUSE instruction on x86, which reduces power consumption and improves performance on hyperthreaded cores by freeing execution resources for the sibling thread.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicBoolean;

public class SpinWaitDemo {
    private static final AtomicBoolean ready = new AtomicBoolean(false);

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread worker = new Thread(() -> {
            while (!ready.get()) {
                Thread.onSpinWait(); // CPU PAUSE instruction
                // Reduces power consumption by ~10%
                // Improves hyperthreading performance by ~30%
            }
            System.out.println("Settlement data ready, processing...");
        });
        worker.start();
        Thread.sleep(100);  // simulate preparation
        ready.set(true);
        worker.join();
    }
}
```

### Does yield() release any locks?

yield() has nothing to do with locks. It does not release any monitor or ReentrantLock. A thread holding a lock that calls yield() may give up its time slice but continues to hold the lock, potentially blocking other threads longer.

### What is the only valid use of yield()?

In testing, yield() between operations increases the chance of context switches, making race conditions more reproducible. It is a debugging aid, not a production tool.

```java
// Testing only: increase chance of interleaving
public void testRaceCondition() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) {
        sharedCounter++;
        Thread.yield();  // encourage context switch to expose race
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Spin versus park is a latency-versus-CPU trade-off. Spinning (yield, onSpinWait) is appropriate for very short waits (nanoseconds to microseconds) where the wake-up cost of parking exceeds the spin time. For longer waits, park the thread (wait, await, LockSupport.park) to save CPU cycles. At National Bank of India, the trade sequencer uses onSpinWait for sub-microsecond critical sections, while all IO-related waits use proper blocking primitives.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How does yield() differ from sleep(0)? sleep(0) may behave similarly on some platforms but semantically means "sleep for zero milliseconds" while yield() means "give up the rest of my time slice." Both are unreliable.
- Does yield() affect thread priority? No. Priority is a separate hint to the scheduler.
- How do virtual threads handle yield? Virtual threads are unmounted from their carrier thread, making yield() effectively a no-op since the scheduler already manages virtual threads cooperatively.
